From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 12:21:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Add a shadow DOM node model and flat-tree traversal

Groundwork for native shadow DOM: a shadow root is a slab node that
lives outside its host's child list (the template_contents pattern),
carrying host back-pointer + open/closed mode. ElementData gains the
shadow_root slot pointing at it. Match arms added where NodeData is
matched exhaustively (inline-box scan tolerates a stray shadow root
rather than panicking the paint; the embedder recovers from unwinds, but
a poisoned paint helps nobody).

DioxusLabs/blitz roadmap #119 lists shadow DOM as wanted but unstarted.
Upstream would likely want this behind an opt-in feature gate, as PR
#478 proposed.

attach_shadow creates the shadow-root node, parents it to the host while
keeping it OUT of host.children (parent pointer preserves the
mark_ancestors_dirty chain across the boundary; absence from children
keeps light-tree walks blind), and mirrors the host's in-document flag
so later appends into the root propagate flags correctly via
add_children_to_parent's existing logic.

Lifecycle holes this also plugs: drop_node_ignoring_parent recurses into
attached shadow trees (else their slab slots leak and freed ids get
recycled under live references); deep_clone_node clears the cloned
host's shadow_root (raw copy would alias the original's tree by node id
- spec says clones never copy shadow roots); and iter_subtree_mut
recurses through shadow roots so insertion/removal processing
(IS_IN_DOCUMENT, id map, stylesheet/image special ops) follows the host
subtree it rides in.

Fill the TShadowRoot/TNode/TElement/selectors::Element shadow stubs so
stylo sees the flat tree: a host's traversal children are its shadow
root's children (unslotted light children vanish until slot assignment
lands), traversal_parent and inheritance_parent cross the transparent
shadow-root node to the host, while parent_element deliberately stops at
the boundary so selector matching stays inside the current tree (gecko's
split: matching is per-scope, inheritance is flat).

The inheritance_parent override is load-bearing: its default is
parent_element, which now stops at the root - without the override
shadow content computes from initial values (observed as black text)
instead of inheriting through the host.

TShadowRoot::style_data stays None for now: scoped stylesheet collection
lands with the author-styles patch; a topology-only root styles like an
empty one (UA rules + inheritance).

html5ever's parse_fragment_for_element calls the sink's elem_name on the
context handle, which expects an element and panics for a shadow root
(nodeType 11 lives in the tree as a non-element container).
set_inner_html now parses into a throwaway element cloned from the
host's tag name - matching the context a browser would use - then adopts
the children into the root and drops the temp. Everything else (scoped
<style> registration, damage) rides the normal append path.

process_added_subtree recurses through shadow trees (needed for
in-document flags and stylesheet ops), which would also register shadow
ids into nodes_to_id, so getElementById could see shadow internals or,
worse, a shadow id could clobber a light-DOM entry and silently return
the wrong element to code that never touches shadow DOM. Registration
now skips nodes inside shadow trees.

Removal is value-checked (only delete the entry when it points at the
node being removed) rather than containment-gated: remove_node detaches
before processing, so a containment walk at removal time misreports
shadow content as light. The value check also stops never-registered
duplicates from evicting a live entry.

:nth-child and friends deposit for_parent() flags on the parent node,
and for a shadow root's direct child that parent is the ShadowRoot,
which carries no selector_flags: the accessor panicked mid-cascade. The
document got a flags field for the same reason; a shadow root does not
need one, because nothing reads parent flags and mutations under a root
restyle through the host's subtree hint.
